12  residential run well interestingly about  eighty-one percent of americans said  that they would or may consider voting  for a third party or independent  presidential candidate in 2012 but i  feel like i should be clear about what  this means in the American electoral  system we have two parties and that  that's unlikely to change so what does  this finding really mean and if it most  likely means that people are receptive  to to considering non-traditional  candidates just you know that the mold  of the democratic candidate or the mold  of the Republican candidate isn't the  only kind of candidate they would  consider now in terms of voting that's a  different story in terms of  receptiveness and considerations that  this is actually quite interesting is it  safe to say that I mean there's a this  might reflect a sense of exhaustion with  the [ __ ] live with the status quo with  the political status quo people are  interested in looking at new new ways of  thinking about politics new ways of  talking about debt things like that we  asked a question of respondents which  party they believed to govern more  responsibly and in fact there was no  consensus and that the plurality  response was about thirty-five percent  was neither neither Republicans nor  Democrats which might be explaining some  of the discontent that is being felt  another interesting fact was that  fifty-eight percent of survey  respondents said that they felt like  there would be no difference or that  they would be better off if Congress  only met every other year well I I  suspect that I would have been in that  camp as well Emily Ekins polling  director of the recent foundation and of  the roop Reason Foundation quarterly  polls that are starting now thanks for  talking to us for reason TV I'm Nick  Gillespie  you  you
 

